/**
  @page fem-spaces Finite element spaces
  @brief Choose a field representation, count its DOFs, and evaluate a discrete field.

  A finite element space combines local elements over a mesh. Its choice
  determines the allowed fields and continuity, while a grid function holds one
  particular field in that space. The @ref fem-ciarlet "Ciarlet guide" explains
  the local construction; this guide follows a runnable comparison of the
  principal scalar spaces.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section fem-spaces-available Available spaces

  | Space | Scalar representation | Continuity and role |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | `P0` | One constant per cell | Discontinuous cellwise data |
  | `P0g` | One constant over the entire mesh | A global parameter or multiplier |
  | `P1` | Vertex-based first-order family | Continuous; affine on affine simplices |
  | `H1` | Prescribed compile-time degree | Continuous higher-order approximation |

  `P0` supplies independent cell values; `P0g` supplies one scalar over the mesh. A
  DG formulation additionally specifies the numerical fluxes and stabilization;
  see @ref guides-variational-dg "DG formulations".

  @section fem-spaces-mesh Construct the spaces on one mesh

  The comparison uses a unit square with eight vertices in each direction.
  `UniformGrid` takes vertex counts, so the mesh has 64 vertices and 98 triangles.
  Its edge-to-cell and cell-to-edge relations are computed before constructing
  spaces that use edge DOFs.

  @snippet Variational/FiniteElementSpaces.cpp spaces-setup

  The scalar quadratic space has one DOF at every vertex and every edge on this
  triangular mesh. There are 161 edges, giving @f$64+161=225@f$ scalar DOFs.
  The counts printed by the program are:

  | Space | Global scalar coefficients |
  | :--- | ---: |
  | `P0` | 98 |
  | `P0g` | 1 |
  | `P1` | 64 |
  | Quadratic `H1` | 225 |
  | Two-component `P1` | 128 real coefficients |
  | Complex scalar `P1` | 64 complex coefficients |

  @snippet Variational/FiniteElementSpaces.cpp spaces-counts

  These are counts before essential boundary conditions or other constraints.
  A local cell's basis count differs from the global space's `getSize()`.

  @section fem-spaces-p0 P0 interpolation

  `P0` stores an independent constant on each cell. Its cellwise gradient is zero,
  while the field can jump across interfaces. In the implementation used here,
  assignment applies point evaluation at the mapped reference-cell center.
  On these affine triangles that point is the cell centroid.

  For P0, interpolation evaluates the function at the centroid, while an @f$L^2@f$
  projection assigns its cell average. Both reproduce constants; their values can
  differ for quadratic and non-polynomial data.

  @section fem-spaces-p1 Polynomial degree

  On an affine triangle, scalar P1 has three local basis functions and represents
  affine functions. On a quadrilateral the vertex-based family is bilinear on
  the reference cell; its physical gradient depends on the reference basis and geometric mapping. Cell
  type and geometry mapping therefore remain part of the approximation contract.

  The `H1` constructor takes its degree through `std::integral_constant`. On
  simplices it uses total-degree polynomial spaces, while tensor-product cells
  use the corresponding tensor-product construction.

  | Cell | Scalar local DOFs at degree @f$k@f$ |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | Segment | @f$k+1@f$ |
  | Triangle | @f$(k+1)(k+2)/2@f$ |
  | Quadrilateral | @f$(k+1)^2@f$ |
  | Tetrahedron | @f$(k+1)(k+2)(k+3)/6@f$ |
  | Hexahedron | @f$(k+1)^3@f$ |

  Field degree and geometric representation are chosen independently. Error
  estimates depend on solution regularity, mesh quality, formulation stability,
  and the consistency of geometry and quadrature.

  @section fem-spaces-gridfunctions Assign and evaluate a field

  The comparison interpolates @f$f(x,y)=x^2+y^2@f$ into P0, P1, and quadratic H1.

  @snippet Variational/FiniteElementSpaces.cpp spaces-fields

  Assignment applies the DOF functionals of the selected space to the prescribed
  function. In particular, the quadratic field should
  reproduce this polynomial on the affine triangular mesh, up to roundoff.

  Evaluation goes through `Geometry::Point`, so the reference location and its
  physical cell are supplied together.

  @snippet Variational/FiniteElementSpaces.cpp spaces-sampling

  The additional points refine the sampling of each existing field while preserving
  its DOFs. The CSV records evaluations at the corresponding physical points.

  @section fem-spaces-visualization Inspect the interpolated fields

  @m_class{rodin-mesh-stages}

  @parblock
  @image html Images/SpaceInterpolation-p0.png "P0 interpolation assigns one constant value to each original triangle."
  @image html Images/SpaceInterpolation-p1.png "P1 interpolation of the same quadratic function."
  @image html Images/SpaceInterpolation-p2.png "Quadratic H1 interpolation of the same function. All three figures use the same color scale."
  @endparblock

  The figures plot the interpolated fields. P0 uses its original cell values;
  P1 and quadratic H1 use the exported within-cell samples.
  For this mesh and sampling grid, the largest sampled errors are approximately
  @f$0.186@f$ for P0, @f$0.0102@f$ for P1, and below @f$3\times10^{-15}@f$ for
  quadratic H1. These maxima are measured over the exported sample points with the mesh and
  prescribed function held fixed. Integrated error norms and convergence rates
  require integration and a sequence of refined meshes.

  An @f$L^2@f$ projection is defined by finding @f$w_h\in V_h@f$ with
  @f$\int_\Omega w_hv_h=\int_\Omega fv_h@f$ for every test function. The coefficients are obtained from the resulting mass-matrix problem. See
  @ref guides-numerical-contracts "numerical contracts".

  @section fem-spaces-vector Field range and coefficient ordering

  Scalar, vector, matrix, and complex ranges are distinct from the spatial
  dimension. A two-dimensional mesh can carry a vector field with another
  component count; the model decides whether that choice is meaningful.
  A vector displacement in the @ref examples-pdes-elasticity "beam example"
  uses one component per spatial direction.

  Entity DOFs and local-to-global indices are obtained from the space. These maps
  specify coefficient ordering for scalar, vector and higher-order fields.

  @section fem-spaces-matrix Extend the field range to matrices

  P0, P0g, P1, and H1 also have componentwise matrix-valued specializations.
  Matrix rows and columns are separate from mesh dimension; symmetry and Voigt scaling are specified by the formulation. These details, tensor
  derivatives, persistence, and backend support are retained in the
  @subpage guides-spatial-matrix "matrix solutions and spatial tensors reference".

  @section fem-spaces-complex Represent complex fields

  `P1<Complex>` assigns complex scalar coefficients to the geometric DOF locations
  of P1.
  The @ref examples-pdes-helmholtz "Helmholtz tutorial" shows a complex solve and
  exports real and imaginary parts as separate real fields.

  @section fem-spaces-mixed Mixed spaces

  The @ref examples-pdes-stokes "Stokes tutorial" uses quadratic velocity,
  linear pressure, and a global P0g multiplier for the pressure mean. The velocity–pressure pairing is selected to satisfy the stability requirements of
  the mixed formulation.

  @section fem-spaces-complete Complete program

  Build the `FiniteElementSpaces` example target. It prints the counts above and
  writes `FiniteElementSpaces.csv` and `ReferenceP1.csv` in the working directory.

  See @ref Variational/FiniteElementSpaces.cpp "FiniteElementSpaces.cpp" and the
  @ref guides-variational "variational guide" for the next step from a known
  field to an unknown field in a weak problem.

  @example Variational/FiniteElementSpaces.cpp
*/
